    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d447">

                <pb facs="0680" n="578" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg578"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">883.101/4</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Fish</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 204</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-02-15">February 15,
                        1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that since
                    mailing my despatch No. 203 of February 14, 1935, the Legation has learned that
                    the French Government has likewise informed the Egyptian Government that it
                    reserves all rights regarding the changes effected in the administration of
                    Alexandria by the “unilateral procedure” of the Decree-Law of January 1, 1935,
                    and the Ministerial Orders of January 2, 1935.</p>
                <p>The Legation also hears that some of the foreign residents of Alexandria may
                    decide to test the validity of the new municipal administration by causing
                    themselves to be sued in the Mixed Courts for refusal to pay taxes.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bert Fish</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
